WEBVTT

00:00:06.465 --> 00:00:07.716
[classical music plays]

00:00:09.426 --> 00:00:12.012
[Jerry] <i>This is gonna be</i>
<i>a very special day today.</i>

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:16.433
<i>Today we are driving</i>
<i>the 2004 Porsche Carrera GT.</i>

00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:22.189
<i>There are things,</i>
<i>there are special things.</i>

00:00:22.898 --> 00:00:24.691
<i>And then there is this thing.</i>

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:32.032
<i>It was introduced to the public</i>
<i>in the year 2000</i>

00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:34.952
<i>on a rainy morning in Paris at 6:00 a.m.</i>

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:42.251
<i>It was originally intended to be</i>
<i>a race car for Le Mans,</i>

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:44.127
<i>but they decided not to do the race</i>

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.798
<i>and instead just kind of tame it down,</i>
<i>make it a streetcar.</i>

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:50.050
<i>That was a good, crazy idea.</i>

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.010
<i>Credit for the design and execution</i>

00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:55.556
<i>goes to Porsche superstars</i>
<i>Grant Larson and Tony Hatter,</i>

00:00:55.639 --> 00:00:57.182
<i>one from US, the other UK.</i>

00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:01.520
<i>The similarity between great car design</i>
<i>and great comedy is in comedy,</i>

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:05.023
<i>when you have a great joke, </i>
<i>it has to be the exact perfect words,</i>

00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:07.484
<i>each one in the exact right place.</i>

00:01:07.568 --> 00:01:09.570
I said, "What's the matter,
is there somebody else?"

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:11.488
She looked at me.
She said, "There must be."

00:01:11.572 --> 00:01:13.198
[Jerry] <i>Or it just doesn't quite work.</i>

00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:15.701
<i>In car design, every line and shape</i>

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:18.453
<i>has to connect perfectly</i>
<i>with every other line and shape,</i>

00:01:18.537 --> 00:01:20.497
<i>or it just doesn't look quite right.</i>

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:23.500
<i>This car looks quite right.</i>

00:01:24.418 --> 00:01:27.421
<i>Most cars, like, have some good angles,</i>
<i>some not good angles.</i>

00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:30.257
<i>This car, every way you look at it,</i>

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.301
<i>nothing cheesy, nothing juvenile,</i>

00:01:32.384 --> 00:01:34.970
<i>trying too hard, "Please, like me."</i>

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:38.181
<i>It's just a perfect, mature, exciting,</i>
<i>beautiful shape.</i>

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.644
<i>These cars cost a lot when they were new,</i>
<i>cost a lot to maintain.</i>

00:01:42.728 --> 00:01:44.938
<i>Is it worth it? Yeah, it's worth it!</i>

00:01:45.022 --> 00:01:46.857
<i>Life's a pain in the ass, too.</i>
<i>Is that worth it?</i>

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.774
[revving]

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:51.945
<i>The engine is a normally aspirated V10.</i>
<i>Not eight, not twelve. Ten!</i>

00:01:52.404 --> 00:01:53.864
<i>But it's the sound of it.</i>

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.325
-[revving]
<i>-This engine starts or revs,</i>

00:01:56.408 --> 00:01:59.620
<i>it's like ten mountain lions all clearing</i>
<i>their throat simultaneously...</i>

00:01:59.703 --> 00:02:01.038
[revving]

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.957
<i>...and then going,</i>
<i>"All right. Let's go kill something."</i>

00:02:04.291 --> 00:02:05.667
<i>So many cool details.</i>

00:02:05.751 --> 00:02:08.003
<i>The stick shift is made</i>
<i>of polished birchwood</i>

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:10.881
<i>to resemble the legendary 917 race car.</i>

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.883
<i>Why did they use birchwood?</i>

00:02:13.592 --> 00:02:15.469
[singing voice] <i>It's lighter.</i>

00:02:16.595 --> 00:02:18.889
<i>I need this car today </i>

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.350
<i>because it's one of the most special</i>
<i>Porsches of all time,</i>

00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:25.187
<i>and I am going to visit with one</i>
<i>of the most special comedians of all time.</i>

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.148
-Now, ladies and gentlemen...
-[Jerry] <i>The legendary, spectacular,</i>

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:29.066
<i>thrillingly funny...</i>

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.150
Eddie Murphy!

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:31.735
-[bleep] you.
-[laughter]

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:34.112
-[Jerry] <i>Eddie Murphy and I both...</i>
-Born in Brooklyn,

00:02:34.196 --> 00:02:36.573
-then move out the Long Island.
-You move out the Long Island.

00:02:36.657 --> 00:02:40.118
<i>We started at the Comic Strip together</i>
<i>in Manhattan the exact same week,</i>

00:02:40.202 --> 00:02:42.621
<i>-July of 1976.</i>
-[cheering]

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:45.582
<i>We've had very different career paths,</i>
<i>but still a lot in common.</i>

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.210
<i>Here's a line up card</i>
<i>from a show Eddie and I did</i>

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.671
<i>at a Chinese restaurant in 1979.</i>

00:02:50.754 --> 00:02:52.547
<i>Both of our names misspelled,</i>

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:53.715
<i>and now look at us.</i>

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:57.219
<i>Here we are, all grown up, a couple</i>
<i>of Long Island comedy gunslingers.</i>

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:59.304
-[laughing]
<i>-I could not be more excited</i>

00:02:59.388 --> 00:03:01.056
<i>to be hanging out with him today.</i>

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:02.808
-Hey!
-[phone rings]

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:04.893
-[Jerry] <i>Hey, man.</i>
-[Eddie] <i>'Sup, man?</i>

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:06.853
[Jerry] <i>Would you mind</i>
<i>if I use the bathroom?</i>

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:08.397
[Eddie] <i>You got a take a one or two?</i>

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.066
[Jerry] <i>Two? God, I don't like that.</i>
<i>That is horrible.</i>

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:11.983
[Eddie laughs]

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.447
[Jerry] <i>I think you should come along</i>
<i>for this one.</i>

00:03:18.699 --> 00:03:22.285
<i>On a very special</i>
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:27.582
Come on, pee. Do you really have to pee?

00:03:27.666 --> 00:03:28.500
[laughs]

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:30.001
Who pees as a joke?

00:03:30.544 --> 00:03:33.088
-Wow. What is this? A Porsche?
-Yeah.

00:03:33.171 --> 00:03:34.673
-Carrera GT.
-Ah.

00:03:34.756 --> 00:03:36.883
This was one of their most exotic cars.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.970
This car reminds me of you
because of the perfection.

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.847
-Ah.
-It's got perfection.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.473
This car is together like you.

00:03:43.557 --> 00:03:44.808
Yeah, man. Thank you.

00:03:49.855 --> 00:03:51.940
-[Jerry] Have you had coffee yet today?
-[Eddie] No.

00:03:52.023 --> 00:03:52.941
Do you like coffee?

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:54.526
-Yeah, I drink it everyday.
-Me too.

00:03:56.737 --> 00:03:58.822
You must've been making people laugh

00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:00.782
when you were a kid.

00:04:00.866 --> 00:04:03.201
You know, the first time I heard
a group of people laughing,

00:04:03.285 --> 00:04:04.619
I might have been, like...

00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:06.079
like eight or nine.

00:04:06.163 --> 00:04:07.372
We were on the bus...

00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:11.418
coming from a McCarren Pool in Brooklyn.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.295
And everybody that got off the bus,

00:04:13.545 --> 00:04:16.548
I was kind of, like,
doing like what they sound like,

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:18.550
you know, and where they were going.

00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:20.761
And everybody on the bus
was kind of laughing.

00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:22.804
And they realized each time
they got off the bus,

00:04:22.888 --> 00:04:24.806
I was gonna be doing it about them,

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:27.100
I realized
that the whole bus was laughing.

00:04:27.893 --> 00:04:30.479
-That's the first time it was a group.
-Right.

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:33.064
And when I got off the bus, they clapped.

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:34.649
-[Eddie chuckles]
-Right.

00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:37.110
And that's a big thing when you're a kid.

00:04:38.612 --> 00:04:40.864
But I still wasn't going,
"I'm gonna be a comedian."

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:42.532
-No.
-I was just like,

00:04:42.616 --> 00:04:44.576
I knew I could make
a group of people laugh.

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:46.161
I was once on a tour bus

00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:49.039
and the tour guide got off,
and I took the microphone,

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.625
and I said,
"On your left, you have my right,

00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:54.252
and on your right, you'll have my left."

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:55.796
[Eddie laughs]

00:04:57.005 --> 00:04:58.632
I was a kind of a loner, though.

00:04:58.715 --> 00:04:59.883
-I didn't have...
-Oh, really?

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.052
...a group of people
that I hung out with, yeah.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.429
-Mostly, I was by myself.
-Wow!

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:05.764
Me too.

00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:09.518
Long Island's a funny place.

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:11.770
-There's nothing to do there but be funny.
-Yeah.

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:14.898
Or accumulate experiences
that will one day be funny.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.609
I like Long Island. It was nice
when I was living out there.

00:05:17.692 --> 00:05:20.278
It was nice. Yeah, I liked it, too.

00:05:20.362 --> 00:05:23.698
Yeah, at the time I thought everyone
was a [bleep] idiot in Long Island.

00:05:23.782 --> 00:05:25.116
[both laugh]

00:05:29.538 --> 00:05:32.666
A manager that I had found
in the Yellow Pages in...

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:34.668
in Levittown.

00:05:34.751 --> 00:05:36.127
King Broder his name was.

00:05:36.211 --> 00:05:39.005
-Yeah. I remember that guy. Yes, right!
-King Broder.

00:05:39.089 --> 00:05:40.841
King Broder's actually
on <i>Saturday Night Live.</i>

00:05:40.924 --> 00:05:43.176
I used to do a character called Gumby.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.053
I remember Gumby.

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:46.471
And he's kind of loosely based

00:05:46.555 --> 00:05:47.889
-on King Broder.
-Oh, yes.

00:05:47.973 --> 00:05:51.059
I can buy this network.
I'm a very rich piece of gum, baby.

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:52.727
"Betty Rubble was a whore."

00:05:53.144 --> 00:05:55.397
You know, "I ain't know Betty Rubble."

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.982
[laughter]

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:00.318
I never thought
I would be living in California.

00:06:00.861 --> 00:06:02.445
-Really?
-Never.

00:06:02.529 --> 00:06:04.781
-'Cause you had a place in Jersey.
-Yeah, for years.

00:06:04.865 --> 00:06:07.701
I don't dislike it out here,
but I never thought I would live out here.

00:06:07.784 --> 00:06:09.119
And how's it going?

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:10.745
-I'm cool with it. I like it.
-Yeah.

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:12.205
I won't be missing stuff.

00:06:12.873 --> 00:06:14.040
It's kind of like, you know,

00:06:14.124 --> 00:06:16.084
-whatever's in front of me.
-Right.

00:06:16.501 --> 00:06:19.004
I don't do a lot of pining 
for the old days and...

00:06:19.087 --> 00:06:20.839
-Right.
-The good old days and that stuff.

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:23.049
-That's pretty--
-Well, that happens, uh...

00:06:23.341 --> 00:06:24.342
It's kind of like..

00:06:24.426 --> 00:06:25.468
That's very good.

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:29.222
A lot of people put a lot of work
into trying to get to that place.

00:06:29.306 --> 00:06:32.350
Right now is the best it's ever been.
And it only gets better.

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:33.852
[upbeat music plays]

00:06:35.729 --> 00:06:38.315
<i>Raw, your </i>special from '87,

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:40.442
it's so polished, it's so tight.

00:06:41.276 --> 00:06:44.029
-And...
-My suit that I was wearing?

00:06:44.112 --> 00:06:45.822
I can talk about the suit, too.

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:50.452
-I told you that's my biggest regret is...
 -Yes.

00:06:50.535 --> 00:06:53.079
...stopping.
Like, I used to just go and do it.

00:06:53.455 --> 00:06:54.289
Right.

00:06:54.623 --> 00:06:56.583
Stopping and letting that muscle atrophy.

00:06:56.666 --> 00:06:58.501
-Yes. 
-It's my only regret.

00:06:58.585 --> 00:07:01.713
You don't like going up unless you really
have something on stage, you know?

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:05.091
-I wouldn't go out on tour without an act.
-Right.

00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:07.719
The hardest thing about getting back
on stage again

00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:09.846
-is when you don't have anything.
-Right.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.265
I don't have any... anything.

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:14.684
That's like a... a scary thought.

00:07:17.604 --> 00:07:21.232
Nobody could believe that you could be
on stage and not feel comfortable.

00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:22.609
But you can.

00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:24.361
-All comics are.
-Yeah.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.447
Even when you got it all the way down,
that's part of it.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:31.409
The times I've gotten on stage
and felt totally loose

00:07:31.493 --> 00:07:34.287
with no energy
of being uncomfortable at all,

00:07:34.371 --> 00:07:35.580
I-I would do shitty.

00:07:36.206 --> 00:07:38.833
-Yeah, I wouldn't have as strong a set.
-You wouldn't have?

00:07:38.917 --> 00:07:41.252
-No, that doesn't work if you--
-Right. Right.

00:07:41.336 --> 00:07:43.171
You gotta have a little bit of...

00:07:43.713 --> 00:07:45.548
-Yeah.
-"What if this shit doesn't...

00:07:45.632 --> 00:07:47.884
What if they don't get all of this shit?"

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:50.512
I always do best
when I have a little bit of that.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:55.141
I love that I don't know what's there
till I walk out.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.852
I don't know if I'm gonna feel strong
or weak or...

00:07:57.936 --> 00:07:59.312
Or how it's gonna be.

00:08:02.232 --> 00:08:06.069
Then it got different. It got to where
when I was working out stuff,

00:08:06.945 --> 00:08:08.947
it would be in the paper,
like, the next day.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.409
They were on me too much
during the creative process.

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:15.829
I remember, uh,
Joan River's husband had died.

00:08:16.121 --> 00:08:19.666
And I went up at the Comedy Store
and I did like some bit about, you know--

00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:22.168
'Cause she used to do all these jokes
about Edgar, "Oh, this--"

00:08:22.252 --> 00:08:24.546
-Yeah.
-He had committed suicide.

00:08:24.629 --> 00:08:27.590
And I did some joke like,
"He got tired of all them Edgar jokes."

00:08:27.674 --> 00:08:30.677
-Some shit I said that was...
-Right. [laughing]

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.638
It wasn't cool.
And it was in the paper the next day.

00:08:33.722 --> 00:08:35.432
And everyone was outraged and...

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:36.516
-Right.
-Joan was hurt.

00:08:36.599 --> 00:08:38.643
And I was like,
"That wasn't even a bit yet."

00:08:38.727 --> 00:08:39.853
[upbeat music plays]

00:08:42.063 --> 00:08:43.815
When I first came to New York,

00:08:44.149 --> 00:08:46.067
-you and Larry Miller...
-Yeah.

00:08:46.151 --> 00:08:47.652
-...were the popular comics.
-Yes.

00:08:47.736 --> 00:08:50.447
And everybody was 
kind of like doing his cadence.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:51.740
-Or your cadence.
-Right.

00:08:51.823 --> 00:08:55.076
You were the most polished,
and you were funnier than everybody.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.203
-And you were so smart.
-Oh, thank you. Thank you.

00:08:57.287 --> 00:08:59.372
Like, you didn't wanna follow you

00:08:59.914 --> 00:09:01.499
or George Wallace.

00:09:01.583 --> 00:09:03.209
-That's right.
-He was so big.

00:09:03.293 --> 00:09:05.462
"It's a crazy world!" I was like, "Shit."

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:06.379
[chuckles]

00:09:06.463 --> 00:09:07.297
Man.

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:09.758
-'Cause his presence was so big.
-Yes.

00:09:09.841 --> 00:09:12.343
He's still big. And I'm still polished.

00:09:12.427 --> 00:09:13.470
[both laugh]

00:09:15.513 --> 00:09:20.268
You know that you not doing stand-up
drives people crazy. You know that, right?

00:09:20.351 --> 00:09:23.063
The last time I saw Don Rickles,

00:09:23.146 --> 00:09:25.815
and, uh... Rickles was, like,
maybe a month or two

00:09:25.899 --> 00:09:27.192
before he passed away,

00:09:27.275 --> 00:09:30.195
and he just went on and on and on
like that.

00:09:30.278 --> 00:09:32.530
-Like, "You have to do stand-up again.
-Really?

00:09:32.614 --> 00:09:35.116
You have to..."
Just went on and on about it.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.076
I was like, "Wow."

00:09:36.159 --> 00:09:37.911
-I'm gonna do it again. It was just--
-Really?

00:09:37.994 --> 00:09:39.162
Yeah, I just had to...

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.747
You know, everything has to...

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:42.248
be right.

00:09:42.916 --> 00:09:45.043
I gotta get up there
and start working out.

00:09:45.335 --> 00:09:46.169
Really?

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:49.172
I have to get up and work...
The only way you can get, like, an act

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.674
is I gotta go to clubs and work out.

00:09:52.092 --> 00:09:53.218
I'm gonna do that again.

00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:55.845
-You still gotta go to the comedy clubs.
-You still gotta go...

00:09:55.929 --> 00:09:57.180
You could have your own club.

00:09:57.263 --> 00:10:00.517
So you should buy the Comic Strip,
and I'll come work out there.

00:10:01.768 --> 00:10:03.561
If you wanna do that, I'll do it.

00:10:03.645 --> 00:10:06.064
I'll call it Jerry Seinfeld's Comic Strip.

00:10:08.608 --> 00:10:12.028
How did you go about
coming up with your stuff back then?

00:10:12.112 --> 00:10:14.614
Most stuff happens
when I'm talking to somebody,

00:10:14.906 --> 00:10:17.617
and I'll say something,
and it'll be, "Oh, that's funny."

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.619
And I'll write down what I said.

00:10:19.702 --> 00:10:21.412
Usually, from conversations.

00:10:24.332 --> 00:10:25.416
You want some water?

00:10:26.376 --> 00:10:27.836
-You got water in here?
-No.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.590
-There's a lot of homeless out here.
-Yeah.

00:10:32.674 --> 00:10:34.050
I can't use this, but...

00:10:34.843 --> 00:10:36.636
whenever you see homeless,

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:39.848
if you see more than one
and they're talking,

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:41.391
you realize

00:10:42.142 --> 00:10:44.394
one of them is giving
the other one advice.

00:10:45.812 --> 00:10:46.896
[both laugh]

00:10:48.898 --> 00:10:50.275
-You can use that.
-You can?

00:10:50.358 --> 00:10:51.651
Yeah, you can say that.

00:10:52.152 --> 00:10:54.445
What, you thought that'd be
too hard on the homeless?

00:10:54.529 --> 00:10:55.989
-Yeah.
-Nah, that's a very funny joke.

00:10:56.072 --> 00:10:57.949
'Cause any time two people talk,

00:10:58.032 --> 00:11:00.618
one person's telling the other one
what to do. Right?

00:11:00.702 --> 00:11:02.078
-Absolutely.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:11:02.162 --> 00:11:03.496
[Eddie laughs]

00:11:04.289 --> 00:11:06.791
I'll tell you what you should do.
Here's what I've learned.

00:11:08.877 --> 00:11:12.172
I think it's funny when someone
does a really bad movie

00:11:12.547 --> 00:11:15.049
and then the poster's still up
for a few weeks.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:16.593
[both laugh]

00:11:16.676 --> 00:11:18.761
Especially if you're smiling
in your poster,

00:11:18.845 --> 00:11:20.555
if you're like this in the poster...

00:11:22.265 --> 00:11:25.852
The movie came out, didn't do shit,
the poster still be up like...

00:11:26.603 --> 00:11:27.478
[Eddie laughs]

00:11:28.354 --> 00:11:30.523
And on television, the worst is, uh...

00:11:30.899 --> 00:11:31.941
dance... dancing.

00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:33.943
If you've got a show, whatever it is,

00:11:34.027 --> 00:11:38.072
if you do anything like this... Dang!
They'll be like this, "This Wednesday..."

00:11:39.073 --> 00:11:41.618
-They love to get moving and dancing.
-Yeah.

00:11:41.701 --> 00:11:43.953
If you're dancing
on the bottom of the screen,

00:11:44.204 --> 00:11:45.538
it's a bad show.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.461
What do you do first thing
in the morning?

00:11:51.544 --> 00:11:52.503
I, uh...

00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:54.631
eat some, uh...

00:11:55.506 --> 00:11:56.341
prunes...

00:11:56.424 --> 00:11:58.509
Prunes and raisin bran.

00:11:58.801 --> 00:12:00.261
Prunes and raisin bran?

00:12:00.678 --> 00:12:03.014
Well, I know
what the rest of your day's like.

00:12:04.349 --> 00:12:05.642
I'm totally-- I'm lazy.

00:12:05.725 --> 00:12:08.561
I like, more than anything,
I like to be doing nothing

00:12:08.895 --> 00:12:10.605
with my family and my kids.

00:12:10.688 --> 00:12:12.732
-Oh, can you sit still now?
-Absolutely.

00:12:12.815 --> 00:12:14.484
-Really?
-That's what I do mostly.

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:16.361
-Really?
-That's what I do mostly,

00:12:16.444 --> 00:12:17.654
is just do nothing.

00:12:17.987 --> 00:12:19.906
Doing nothing is my favorite dish.

00:12:19.989 --> 00:12:21.866
And I mean, really nothing, like, uh,

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.703
don't watch the news and don't read...
Don't know who's... what's going on.

00:12:25.787 --> 00:12:28.248
-Right.
-Totally, like, disconnect.

00:12:30.959 --> 00:12:34.295
When you go out, do you ever think about,
"I can't wear these shoes

00:12:34.379 --> 00:12:36.005
'cause I might get into a fight.

00:12:36.089 --> 00:12:38.716
I want traction, I don't wanna be able
to slip around.

00:12:39.467 --> 00:12:41.594
I'm ready, I'm always ready. I'm ready.

00:12:41.928 --> 00:12:43.930
Just want you [bleep] to know I'm ready.

00:12:44.013 --> 00:12:45.306
[Jerry laughs]

00:12:45.556 --> 00:12:48.559
You know, if it comes to that,
I'm ready for that.

00:12:48.726 --> 00:12:51.354
I still have it within me to go there."

00:12:51.437 --> 00:12:52.522
[Jerry laughs]

00:12:52.605 --> 00:12:53.564
[Eddie laughs]

00:13:05.285 --> 00:13:06.577
[Jerry laughs]

00:13:21.718 --> 00:13:23.303
I am so happy to see you.

00:13:23.386 --> 00:13:25.221
I... I love you so much.

00:13:25.305 --> 00:13:27.807
And always have. Always have.

00:13:28.182 --> 00:13:29.100
Thanks.

00:13:29.475 --> 00:13:30.310
Yeah, man.

00:13:30.393 --> 00:13:33.021
You've just always been
the magical guy to me.

00:13:33.104 --> 00:13:34.522
-You would just--
-I told you,

00:13:34.605 --> 00:13:36.524
you were always the funniest.

00:13:36.607 --> 00:13:38.693
-Oh, thank you, please.
-You were always the funniest.

00:13:38.776 --> 00:13:41.321
When I started out,
you were the funniest one.

00:13:41.404 --> 00:13:42.447
-Always.
-Hello.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:43.364
Still.

00:13:46.367 --> 00:13:49.162
Do you remember <i>The Big Laugh Off</i> of '78?

00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:50.496
I was in <i>The Big Laugh Off.</i>

00:13:51.873 --> 00:13:55.626
See, the South Queens basketball team
suddenly become undefeated.

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.045
[laughter]

00:13:57.462 --> 00:13:58.504
[applause]

00:13:58.588 --> 00:14:00.214
I hope you had fun. I gotta go home.

00:14:00.298 --> 00:14:01.382
I was there.

00:14:01.466 --> 00:14:04.594
That's where I met Robert Klein.
That night.

00:14:04.886 --> 00:14:07.847
'Cause I have that picture with him,
and I know it was at the Copa.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.433
It's so cool to be able to say
you played the Copa.

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:13.019
-Yeah. Yeah.
-It just sounds really show business-y.

00:14:13.102 --> 00:14:14.604
It's so cruel, these shows,

00:14:14.687 --> 00:14:17.148
where they make people think,
"You've got it!"

00:14:17.482 --> 00:14:20.109
You win the contest and you think,
"Okay, I won.

00:14:20.526 --> 00:14:23.821
They have allowed me
into the entertainment industry."

00:14:24.364 --> 00:14:25.365
No they haven't.

00:14:25.698 --> 00:14:27.450
Yeah, you did well tonight, that's it.

00:14:27.533 --> 00:14:28.618
[jazz plays]

00:14:30.203 --> 00:14:33.206
-[Jerry] And what do watch?
-Have you ever watched the... [laughs]

00:14:33.289 --> 00:14:37.377
the little women, the little midgets...
the little people show?

00:14:37.627 --> 00:14:39.212
I have not, but I've heard about it.

00:14:39.712 --> 00:14:42.882
If you discriminate against them,
they're a minority.

00:14:42.965 --> 00:14:45.510
-Yeah. You can't use the word "midget."
-I know that.

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:49.847
But I said, "Was there a meeting to decide
what are we gonna be called, then?

00:14:50.139 --> 00:14:53.184
Did they have any other... names?"

00:14:53.559 --> 00:14:56.729
-Uh, "petite-eo."
-No. You can't say "petite-eo."

00:14:56.813 --> 00:14:58.439
-[laughs]
-You can't say no shit like that.

00:14:58.523 --> 00:15:00.400
-Nothing... none of that?
-Gotta get rid of that.

00:15:00.483 --> 00:15:02.693
They'll be like,
"That's the same as saying "nigger.'"

00:15:06.614 --> 00:15:09.492
Who did you look at
and went, "Oh, my God!"?

00:15:09.575 --> 00:15:13.037
Richard was the first person that made me
think about being a comedian.

00:15:13.121 --> 00:15:14.997
But Richard was the first one where...

00:15:15.081 --> 00:15:18.626
It was an album that came out in '75
called <i>That Nigger's Crazy.</i>

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:20.128
-Right.
-That album...

00:15:21.129 --> 00:15:25.716
I remember getting it and listening to it
over and over and over

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.261
-and over and over, like, by myself.
-Yeah.

00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:30.930
-And all the time, just over and over.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:15:31.013 --> 00:15:33.766
I was like that with Cosby's
<i>Why Is There Air?</i>

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:36.352
<i>Why Is There Air? </i>drove me crazy.

00:15:36.436 --> 00:15:37.812
I wore it out.

00:15:40.314 --> 00:15:43.025
Where's the first place
you remember standing on a stage?

00:15:43.109 --> 00:15:45.820
At the youth center.
They had a talent contest.

00:15:46.279 --> 00:15:48.865
I had a tape recorder, and I taped it.

00:15:49.073 --> 00:15:51.367
And afterwards,
when I was listening to it,

00:15:51.868 --> 00:15:54.871
it blew my mind 'cause it sounded
like one of the Richard albums.

00:15:54.954 --> 00:15:57.415
Like, an album... It was like,
you could hear people laughing.

00:15:57.498 --> 00:15:59.041
-Right.
-You could hear glass broke,

00:15:59.125 --> 00:16:01.335
like somebody knocked something over.
Could hear all that.

00:16:01.419 --> 00:16:03.754
-Oh, my God.
-And I was in from then.

00:16:04.088 --> 00:16:06.174
You realize your memory is amazing?

00:16:06.674 --> 00:16:07.925
I have a really good memory.

00:16:08.009 --> 00:16:11.137
You remember the name of the Chinese
restaurant gig we did in Jersey?

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:12.138
The Jade Fountain.

00:16:13.014 --> 00:16:14.932
-The Jade Fountain.
-That's crazy.

00:16:15.016 --> 00:16:16.976
The Jade Fountain.
Do you remember that place?

00:16:17.059 --> 00:16:18.603
-Yeah, vaguely.
-And the owner would say,

00:16:18.686 --> 00:16:21.564
"Hey, let me put your picture up here.
I'll give you exposure.

00:16:21.647 --> 00:16:24.400
Give me one of your pictures,
you'll get a lot of exposure."

00:16:24.484 --> 00:16:27.695
Exposure in the lounge
of the Jade Fountain.

00:16:31.199 --> 00:16:32.658
Okay, you're listening to the albums,

00:16:32.742 --> 00:16:35.745
you're making your friends laugh,
you're building, like, a bridge.

00:16:35.828 --> 00:16:38.706
It's like two cliffs to me, right?

00:16:38.789 --> 00:16:40.416
You're building this bridge
from each side.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.211
You keep listening, listening
to the album. "That bit is so funny."

00:16:43.294 --> 00:16:45.087
It makes you laugh. 
You can't believe this guy

00:16:45.171 --> 00:16:46.923
can make you laugh over and over again.

00:16:47.006 --> 00:16:50.301
And then you're making your friends laugh,
and then they meet.

00:16:50.593 --> 00:16:54.764
And you get up on a stage and make people
that don't even know who you are laugh.

00:16:54.847 --> 00:16:57.141
When I passed the audition
at the Comic Strip,

00:16:57.225 --> 00:16:58.726
I had been working out on the island.

00:16:58.809 --> 00:17:00.102
You gotta get off the island.

00:17:00.186 --> 00:17:02.897
When I passed,
I was bragging to everybody,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.858
"You gotta pay five dollars
to hear me now."

00:17:05.942 --> 00:17:08.736
The cover charge was five dollars
at the Comic Strip.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:10.530
-Even though you wasn't getting paid?
-Yeah.

00:17:10.613 --> 00:17:12.698
"So, you gotta pay five dollars
to get me."

00:17:12.782 --> 00:17:14.492
-Yeah. [laughing]
-"I get nothing.

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:17.328
-But you gotta pay to see me."
-Yeah.

00:17:19.914 --> 00:17:21.457
The place was Catch.

00:17:21.666 --> 00:17:23.459
-Catch.
-That was the room you wanted to be in.

00:17:23.543 --> 00:17:24.835
That... that was the place.

00:17:24.919 --> 00:17:27.463
-Freddie Prinze and all those guys.
-Yeah. Rodney and Brenner.

00:17:27.547 --> 00:17:30.174
-That's where they went.
-Yeah, so you really felt like,

00:17:30.258 --> 00:17:31.384
that room really had that.

00:17:31.467 --> 00:17:33.594
Like, look who had been discovered there.

00:17:35.721 --> 00:17:38.349
The most fun I've ever had working
on a movie was with John Landis.

00:17:38.432 --> 00:17:40.518
He's funny, and he directs you.

00:17:40.601 --> 00:17:41.936
Like, he actually was saying,

00:17:42.019 --> 00:17:44.564
-"Say it like this, and do this..."
-Right.

00:17:44.647 --> 00:17:47.692
See, you can say that to a comic.
You can't say that to an actor.

00:17:47.775 --> 00:17:49.569
-What's that?
-"Say it like this."

00:17:49.652 --> 00:17:51.571
-Yeah, he would say that to--
-Comics don't care.

00:17:51.654 --> 00:17:53.197
-Just tell me how to say it.
-Yeah.

00:17:55.241 --> 00:17:57.577
Then it started feeling more like work.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.870
-Well, because the money gets bigger.
-Yeah--

00:17:59.954 --> 00:18:02.665
And the fun gets smaller.
That's the problem.

00:18:02.748 --> 00:18:06.085
Comedy and money, to me,
are antithetical.

00:18:06.168 --> 00:18:09.505
'Cause one of the things that makes things
funny is when you don't have any money.

00:18:09.839 --> 00:18:12.466
I feel like when you add money
to a project,

00:18:12.675 --> 00:18:14.802
you're tampering with the comedy.

00:18:14.885 --> 00:18:17.346
'Cause all of a sudden,
we're building big things,

00:18:17.972 --> 00:18:20.683
taking time... taking the fun out of it.

00:18:20.766 --> 00:18:22.435
And with a little pressure.

00:18:22.518 --> 00:18:25.396
Yeah. Little pressure, little cheap,
little sloppy.

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:28.566
Pressure. Like I said,
when you get ready to go on stage,

00:18:28.649 --> 00:18:30.735
-and you just feel like--
-Right, that little edge.

00:18:30.818 --> 00:18:32.361
-You gotta... That's crucial.
-Yeah.

00:18:35.364 --> 00:18:38.200
The worst I ever did, ever,
I did a jazz club

00:18:38.284 --> 00:18:39.952
out on... in Queens.

00:18:40.161 --> 00:18:42.163
And my Dad had to drive me to the gig.

00:18:42.663 --> 00:18:44.373
And I bombed horribly.

00:18:44.457 --> 00:18:47.668
And I was supposed to have enough money
to get a cab back home,

00:18:47.752 --> 00:18:50.880
and I did so bad that the guy
wouldn't pay me.

00:18:51.297 --> 00:18:52.715
-[laughs]
-Oh, my God.

00:18:53.215 --> 00:18:54.133
Gerald's.

00:18:54.717 --> 00:18:57.345
-I was supposed to get 50 bucks, right?
-Oh, my God. Right.

00:18:57.428 --> 00:18:59.722
So, I was supposed to do two shows.
I go on, he says,

00:18:59.805 --> 00:19:02.350
"Yeah, you gonna do one set,
and then, uh...

00:19:02.433 --> 00:19:04.018
then the band's gonna come on.

00:19:04.101 --> 00:19:05.311
Then you have a break.

00:19:05.394 --> 00:19:08.022
Then, you know, you come on again."
I was like, "Okay."

00:19:08.105 --> 00:19:09.440
So, I go on,

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:10.858
and I was so bad.

00:19:10.941 --> 00:19:12.234
And there was no dressing room.

00:19:12.318 --> 00:19:15.905
So I bombed, then afterwards,
you had to go sit in the room

00:19:15.988 --> 00:19:18.074
with the people you just bombed with.
[laughs]

00:19:20.826 --> 00:19:23.913
You're sitting there, people are
walking by going, "Oh, shit, dude."

00:19:24.789 --> 00:19:26.207
I'm, like, 17.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:27.333
[Jerry laughs]

00:19:27.416 --> 00:19:29.543
Then they'd say, "We're gonna bring
that comedian back."

00:19:29.627 --> 00:19:32.338
And you would hear people go, "Oh, shit!"

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:37.051
And then, afterwards,
I'd go up to Gerald...

00:19:37.426 --> 00:19:38.719
He was a grown man, too.

00:19:38.803 --> 00:19:40.721
It was really mean to be like this to me.

00:19:40.805 --> 00:19:44.225
I went up, I was like, "Yes, I was
supposed to get $50 for the night."

00:19:44.308 --> 00:19:47.478
He turned around and said, "You better
get the [bleep] out of my face."

00:19:47.561 --> 00:19:48.646
[both laugh]

00:19:51.148 --> 00:19:55.736
Then my father had to drive out to Queens
at two in the morning and drive me back.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.198
"Oh, you a comedian, huh? Comedian...
Now, you gonna get you a job!

00:19:59.281 --> 00:20:01.075
And you gonna get out
at nine in the morning,

00:20:01.158 --> 00:20:03.703
and you come back after five,
[bleep] comedian!

00:20:03.786 --> 00:20:05.371
Comedian my ass!"

00:20:10.126 --> 00:20:12.294
-I'm a careful man.
-Yes, you are.

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:13.921
But not in your comedy.

00:20:14.004 --> 00:20:17.133
You were definitely a fearless comedian.

00:20:21.721 --> 00:20:23.681
I wouldn't say I was fearless, though.

00:20:24.056 --> 00:20:26.267
I wasn't just I'd just say anything,
though.

00:20:26.684 --> 00:20:28.352
You too. You were really confident.

00:20:28.436 --> 00:20:30.980
-Not like you.
-That's what you had... I'm telling you.

00:20:31.063 --> 00:20:35.025
You were the first comic
that seemed like you knew it.

00:20:35.109 --> 00:20:37.278
Like, you got it 
and you seemed like you had--

00:20:37.361 --> 00:20:40.281
Well, if I did, that was just me
acting like that,

00:20:40.364 --> 00:20:43.409
'cause I know that's what works.
But to me, when I watched you...

00:20:45.161 --> 00:20:48.372
I thought,
"This guy's not scared of anything."

00:20:48.456 --> 00:20:49.957
Nothing is more fun...

00:20:50.624 --> 00:20:52.752
-than seeing someone you know bomb...
-Yes, yes.

00:20:52.835 --> 00:20:53.669
[laughs]

00:20:53.753 --> 00:20:56.338
And them knowing you're in the audience
and watching them.

00:20:56.422 --> 00:20:57.298
And it also...

00:20:57.381 --> 00:21:00.968
it makes the experience more fun
for the comic that's... that's on stage.

00:21:01.051 --> 00:21:02.553
At any time I'm dying,

00:21:03.095 --> 00:21:06.474
and it's somebody in the back,
that knowing laugh,

00:21:06.557 --> 00:21:08.768
-that always makes it easier to bomb.
-Right.

00:21:10.019 --> 00:21:12.396
When was the last time
you had to use Raid?

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:13.314
[laughs]

00:21:13.397 --> 00:21:15.107
-[chuckles]
-[Jerry] It's been a while.

00:21:15.483 --> 00:21:18.986
And, by the way, I'm not interested
in any other brands. It's Raid.

00:21:19.069 --> 00:21:21.530
-D-CON works, too.
-Forget d-CON.

00:21:21.614 --> 00:21:24.366
All these companies that are
in second place, that they hang in there.

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:26.202
-Why don't you just quit?
-D-Con is in second.

00:21:26.285 --> 00:21:29.538
Yes. Avis, Colgate... Just quit.

00:21:29.622 --> 00:21:31.582
-RC Cola.
-[laughs] RC Cola!

00:21:31.665 --> 00:21:35.127
How sad is it to work at these places?
You're never gonna catch 'em.

00:21:35.211 --> 00:21:38.255
Pepsi. You're never gonna catch 'em.
[laughs]

00:21:40.341 --> 00:21:41.926
Did you ever have somebody come on stage?

00:21:42.009 --> 00:21:45.012
Yeah, I had somebody come on stage once.
He was so formal.

00:21:45.095 --> 00:21:47.932
"This young whippersnapper
stepped over the line.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:51.018
You're a marvelous audience.
Thank you and good night."

00:21:51.101 --> 00:21:52.269
[Jerry] Oh, my God.

00:21:52.353 --> 00:21:54.522
Oh, and how we laughed on the way home.

00:21:54.814 --> 00:21:56.440
We just laughed and laughed.

00:21:56.524 --> 00:21:58.609
What people don't understand about us is,

00:21:59.026 --> 00:22:01.821
there's nothing that's not funny to us.
Nothing.

00:22:02.613 --> 00:22:04.365
All this careful thing that...

00:22:04.657 --> 00:22:05.574
that's just...

00:22:06.242 --> 00:22:07.827
for the audience and for the public.

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:10.704
Amongst ourselves,
we don't care about anything.

00:22:10.788 --> 00:22:14.083
Somebody died, it's funny 
two minutes after they died.

00:22:14.166 --> 00:22:15.501
We see the joke...

00:22:15.584 --> 00:22:17.086
-Yes.
-...in everything.

00:22:17.169 --> 00:22:19.421
I've had people say to me,
"So-and-so died,"

00:22:19.505 --> 00:22:21.382
and I went,
"Eh! That's enough of him anyway."

