...you couldn't [bleep] with Bubbles.
He had Bubbles in a cage.

00:33:27.547 --> 00:33:29.382
-Like, "Don't go near him."
-[Jerry laughs]

00:33:29.466 --> 00:33:31.885
[giggles] "Don't go over there, Eddie."

00:33:32.427 --> 00:33:33.762
Did you go to his house?

00:33:33.845 --> 00:33:35.138
Yeah, a few times.

00:33:35.222 --> 00:33:38.350
And the chimp would be, "Aah!"
Going crazy. "Is that Bubbles?"

00:33:38.433 --> 00:33:41.353
And he said,
"Yes. Don't go near the cage." [giggles]

00:33:41.436 --> 00:33:44.606
"Really? I thought Bubbles was..."
"Mm-mm, don't go over there."

00:33:44.689 --> 00:33:45.524
[both laugh]

00:33:49.778 --> 00:33:52.697
Who are these comics
that they have painted on the wall?

00:33:52.781 --> 00:33:54.407
I don't know, but I better be one of 'em.

00:33:57.285 --> 00:33:58.411
That looks like me.

00:33:58.495 --> 00:34:00.664
Like, that could be me,
it could be someone else.

00:34:00.747 --> 00:34:03.124
Could be. These are horrible, Eddie.

00:34:03.208 --> 00:34:05.335
-These are the worst caricatures...
-Which one is you?

00:34:05.418 --> 00:34:06.253
I don't know.

00:34:06.336 --> 00:34:08.797
-I don't wanna be in this thing.
-[Eddie laughs]

00:34:12.968 --> 00:34:14.844
You're right about the smell.

00:34:15.387 --> 00:34:17.347
It's really strong. [laughs]

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:20.600
Well, it's that comedy club smell.
I think it's the... it's the bar,

00:34:20.684 --> 00:34:23.144
and whatever's happening
at a chemical level.

00:34:23.228 --> 00:34:25.272
And your body secretes, what is it?

00:34:25.355 --> 00:34:27.524
-Endorphins, pheromones, or whatever.
-[laughs]

00:34:27.607 --> 00:34:31.194
That shit, a group of people
doing that in the room all the time...

00:34:31.278 --> 00:34:32.737
-Right.
-...is gonna leave a smell.

00:34:32.821 --> 00:34:34.531
-For years and years.
-Years and years.

00:34:34.614 --> 00:34:35.907
It's different from a bar.

00:34:35.991 --> 00:34:37.367
It's different from a bar.

00:34:37.450 --> 00:34:40.704
-It's that other thing.
-It's like a bar and a living room

00:34:40.787 --> 00:34:42.038
soaked in beer.

00:34:42.122 --> 00:34:43.957
And a hint of piss, too.

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:46.710
A Hint of Piss is a good name
for a comedy club.

00:34:48.795 --> 00:34:50.714
I remember on the weekend
I'd go to the early show

00:34:50.797 --> 00:34:52.299
at the East Side Comedy Club.

00:34:52.382 --> 00:34:56.261
Then I would get to the last spot
in the early show

00:34:56.469 --> 00:34:58.263
at the, uh, Rainy Nighthouse.

00:34:58.346 --> 00:35:01.933
Then I'd go to the Comic Strip
and do the first spot in the late show.

00:35:02.017 --> 00:35:05.020
-Wow.
-And then drive back out to Long Island

00:35:05.103 --> 00:35:08.398
and do a last spot in the late...
Do like five or six sets, you know.

00:35:09.608 --> 00:35:12.444
Did you ever see Pryor get up
in a little club?

00:35:12.652 --> 00:35:14.988
You know, I had a strange relationship
with Richard.

00:35:15.071 --> 00:35:17.073
Back when I broke,

00:35:17.824 --> 00:35:23.038
the town was still, like, doing
like a one black guy at a time thing.

00:35:23.413 --> 00:35:27.125
And... so, when I showed up,
Richard kind of had this...

00:35:27.375 --> 00:35:31.588
There was this feeling like,
"Oh, this is the new-- the new one is."

00:35:31.755 --> 00:35:34.549
And Richard kind of felt threatened
like, "Oh, now..."

00:35:34.633 --> 00:35:36.760
So, Richard would get weird. It'd be like,

00:35:36.843 --> 00:35:39.596
if I found out Richard was gonna be
somewhere and we all went,

00:35:39.679 --> 00:35:42.474
Richard would pull up and go like,
"Who's in there?"

00:35:42.557 --> 00:35:45.185
And he'd be like, "Oh, so Eddie Murphy's
over there." He would leave.

00:35:45.268 --> 00:35:46.519
-Really?
-Yeah, he'd be like,

00:35:46.603 --> 00:35:48.980
not want to work out in front of me.
It was strange.

00:35:49.064 --> 00:35:50.857
-Oh, man.
-And I just wanted to puppy dog him.

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:51.983
-I was just like...
-Yeah.

00:35:52.067 --> 00:35:54.486
I found out all this out,
like, afterwards, that,

00:35:54.569 --> 00:35:55.779
"Really, he would do that?"

00:35:56.196 --> 00:35:59.199
He had a weird "I'm threatened"...

00:35:59.991 --> 00:36:01.660
Like, there's a changing of the guards,

00:36:01.743 --> 00:36:03.662
-or this new thing is gonna happen.
-Right, right.

00:36:03.745 --> 00:36:06.623
That was the weirdness with Cosby as well
in the early days.

00:36:06.706 --> 00:36:10.168
He thought I was this new thing,
and they felt threatened by it.

00:36:10.251 --> 00:36:13.171
Yes. What's so funny to me,
when I think back on it,

00:36:13.254 --> 00:36:16.257
how funny it was that he thought

00:36:16.675 --> 00:36:21.763
he had the power to tell you to work
the way he wanted you to work.

00:36:22.597 --> 00:36:25.517
-What crazy fantasy was that?
-That's just ego.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:26.935
-Yeah.
-That ego.

00:36:27.018 --> 00:36:28.269
-[Jerry laughs]
-Yeah.

00:36:28.353 --> 00:36:29.688
-'Cause, see, he...
-Remember that?

00:36:29.771 --> 00:36:31.648
-I'm sure you remember that.
-It was his ego.

00:36:31.731 --> 00:36:35.110
I even in the moment thought, "Is he
out of his mind, telling him how to work?

00:36:35.193 --> 00:36:36.695
He's gonna work how he wants to work."

00:36:36.778 --> 00:36:38.029
Because he did that,

00:36:38.113 --> 00:36:40.532
you know, I was like...
That's one of things that makes...

00:36:40.615 --> 00:36:43.576
He had a weird thing with me
that he didn't have with other comics,

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:45.787
'cause I've heard other people
tell stories about,

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:49.457
"Oh, I had this with him, and he said
this to me. He gave me this advice."

00:36:49.541 --> 00:36:53.253
And the stuff that he said to me
was the exact opposite.

00:36:53.336 --> 00:36:55.255
And it was mean, it was like...

00:36:55.338 --> 00:36:56.423
I did this bit about...

00:36:56.506 --> 00:36:58.633
Somebody heckled me and I said,
"Shut the [bleep] up

00:36:58.717 --> 00:37:01.136
-before I throw my wallet over there."
-Right.

00:37:01.219 --> 00:37:02.178
And he called me up,

00:37:02.262 --> 00:37:05.682
"You can't talk about how much money
you have on stage."

00:37:05.765 --> 00:37:06.933
He's taking it out of context.

00:37:07.016 --> 00:37:09.602
He was like, "Well, you know,
I don't come to see people's show.

00:37:09.686 --> 00:37:13.022
And I know you like Richard." 
And I was like, "Yeah..."

00:37:13.106 --> 00:37:16.067
Then he told me,
"I'm in Atlantic City this weekend.

00:37:16.151 --> 00:37:19.070
If you-- You should come and see
how it's supposed to be done.

00:37:19.154 --> 00:37:20.864
-Oh, man.
-You shouldn't get on the stage

00:37:20.947 --> 00:37:22.323
unless you have something to say."

00:37:22.657 --> 00:37:24.826
-That's what he told me. I was like...
-Wow.

00:37:26.911 --> 00:37:28.204
He wasn't nice.

00:37:28.288 --> 00:37:31.458
He wasn't doing that with everybody.
He did that with me, specifically.

00:37:31.541 --> 00:37:33.418
-Right.
-He was shitty with me.

00:37:35.837 --> 00:37:37.005
[Eddie] Is that a homeless guy

00:37:37.088 --> 00:37:39.299
-sitting in front of the Moustache Cafe?
-Yeah.

00:37:39.883 --> 00:37:41.468
[Eddie] Wow. You got a lot of--

00:37:41.551 --> 00:37:44.012
You know who homeless people
must have contempt for?

00:37:44.095 --> 00:37:44.929
Campers.

00:37:45.013 --> 00:37:46.055
[Jerry laughs]

00:37:46.139 --> 00:37:48.600
It'd be like, "So, this is...
it's a [bleep] joke to y'all.

00:37:48.683 --> 00:37:50.810
-[Jerry laughs]
-You just playing around. I'm out here."

00:37:50.894 --> 00:37:52.812
"Oh, so you think it's fun
to hang outside."

00:37:52.896 --> 00:37:55.815
"You're playing around, huh?
In your little tent, [bleep].

00:37:55.899 --> 00:37:57.734
-I'm out here for real."
-[Jerry laughs]

00:37:57.817 --> 00:37:59.694
-Can you do that bit?
-Absolutely.

00:37:59.778 --> 00:38:01.112
[laughs] All right.

00:38:01.196 --> 00:38:04.616
You can do that bit, and I'd get a kick
out of seeing you do that and being like,

00:38:04.699 --> 00:38:06.242
"I gave him that bit."

00:38:06.326 --> 00:38:07.827
I'd get such a kick out of it.

00:38:11.456 --> 00:38:14.125
When I used to finish my sets
at the Comic Strip

00:38:14.209 --> 00:38:17.587
and I would drive back out to Massapequa,
I'd put disco on.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:18.630
You like disco?

00:38:18.713 --> 00:38:19.714
I love disco.

00:38:19.798 --> 00:38:22.050
It was always so positive and upbeat.

00:38:22.133 --> 00:38:23.092
Don't rock the boat.

00:38:23.176 --> 00:38:25.303
-[laughing] Don't rock the boat, baby.
-[laughs]

00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:29.015
There was nothing like those sets, right?

00:38:29.265 --> 00:38:30.558
-When it would go well?
-Nothing.

00:38:30.642 --> 00:38:33.269
And then, three or four comics
would go to a diner...

00:38:33.478 --> 00:38:34.312
-afterwards.
-Yeah.

00:38:34.395 --> 00:38:35.522
And then, it's just...

00:38:35.605 --> 00:38:37.565
-You laugh till your throat hurts.
-Yeah.

00:38:37.899 --> 00:38:39.776
And I realize it was fun then, too.

00:38:39.859 --> 00:38:41.986
It was not like, "Oh, yeah,
I didn't realize I was..."

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:44.572
Like, I realize
I was having a ball that night.

00:38:44.656 --> 00:38:45.615
Right.

00:38:47.992 --> 00:38:50.119
-'Cause once you've made somebody laugh...
-Yeah.

00:38:50.203 --> 00:38:51.746
-...you've got 'em forever.
-Right.

00:38:51.830 --> 00:38:53.873
-If you really made them laugh...
-Yeah.

00:38:53.957 --> 00:38:55.333
...they always think you're funny.

00:38:55.416 --> 00:38:57.669
So, why is it so... still so hard?

00:38:58.127 --> 00:38:59.921
-I don't find--
-That's in your mind.

00:39:00.004 --> 00:39:01.464
-It is in my mind.
-It's in your mind.

00:39:01.548 --> 00:39:03.967
-It didn't feel that easy to me.
-It's hard because you--

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:05.426
You want to be good,

00:39:05.510 --> 00:39:06.845
that's why you're a great comic.

00:39:06.928 --> 00:39:10.473
'Cause you always wanna [bleep] be good,
and you want it to be better.

00:39:10.557 --> 00:39:11.808
-And you wanna get...
-Right.

00:39:11.891 --> 00:39:15.228
So, that's in your mind,
where it's like, "Oh, the pressure and..."

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:17.772
But the audience is just happy to see you.

00:39:19.816 --> 00:39:22.902
And I stopped doing it. That was one
of things that was shitty, me being

00:39:22.986 --> 00:39:24.028
on stage and...

00:39:24.112 --> 00:39:26.239
I think I might have even said like....

00:39:26.489 --> 00:39:29.409
"I just could just stand here
and you [bleep] guys would laugh it in."

00:39:29.492 --> 00:39:32.495
And I remember I had times
where I just... I just stood there

00:39:32.579 --> 00:39:35.540
-for, you know, ten minutes, and just...
-Right.

00:39:35.623 --> 00:39:36.499
And they would laugh.

00:39:36.583 --> 00:39:39.043
And you just stand there,
and they'd giggle again.

00:39:39.127 --> 00:39:42.088
And then you'd make, like, a little face,
like, "Yah!"

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:45.592
And you literally could...
you literally could just stand there.

00:39:46.175 --> 00:39:48.469
For the ones that really, really came out 
to see you.

00:39:48.553 --> 00:39:49.637
Yeah. Right, right.

00:39:49.721 --> 00:39:53.975
There's no luckier person in show business
than the comedian.

00:39:54.475 --> 00:39:55.810
You can make a living...

00:39:56.769 --> 00:39:58.021
making people laugh.

00:39:58.104 --> 00:39:59.105
I agree.

00:39:59.272 --> 00:40:02.275
That's why I don't want any awards for it.

00:40:06.905 --> 00:40:08.865
How many kids you got
living in this house?

00:40:08.948 --> 00:40:09.991
-Three.
-Three.

00:40:10.491 --> 00:40:11.534
That's nice.

00:40:11.618 --> 00:40:12.994
Getting ready to be four.

00:40:13.536 --> 00:40:15.663
-My other kids are grown-ups.
-Right.

00:40:15.747 --> 00:40:16.623
In their 20s.

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:19.792
But is there anything better
than coming home and the kids are home?

00:40:19.876 --> 00:40:20.960
-Nothing.
-Nothing.

00:40:21.044 --> 00:40:23.713
But now, it's like,
 the older kids have moved out.

00:40:23.796 --> 00:40:25.798
But my house is like, uh...

00:40:27.175 --> 00:40:29.677
the center of it all.
Everybody comes to the house.

00:40:29.761 --> 00:40:31.429
-Right.
-They're always at the house.

00:40:31.512 --> 00:40:32.931
-That's nice.
-Yeah, I love it.

00:40:33.014 --> 00:40:34.474
And is that a Sunday thing?

00:40:34.557 --> 00:40:35.725
Just... all the time.

00:40:35.808 --> 00:40:37.685
-All the time?
-Yeah, my house is the...

00:40:37.769 --> 00:40:41.522
The center of, you know,
our little world, is my house.

00:40:41.606 --> 00:40:42.774
-It's the greatest.
-Yeah.

00:40:42.857 --> 00:40:45.735
But once they're 15 or 16,
their social life takes off.

00:40:45.818 --> 00:40:47.236
They're moving around, socializing.

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:49.489
-But when they move out, it's different.
-Oh, yeah?

00:40:49.572 --> 00:40:51.449
Yeah, it's one thing
when they go and socialize

00:40:51.532 --> 00:40:53.117
-and they always come back to you.
-Right.

00:40:53.201 --> 00:40:55.119
-When they move out for real...
-Yeah.

00:40:55.203 --> 00:40:58.998
...you just cry like...
Oh, you cry, cry like an old grandmother.

00:40:59.082 --> 00:41:03.252
When they leave, you go in the room
and [bleep] fall apart.

00:41:06.714 --> 00:41:08.216
Hey, did you get that, uh...

00:41:09.425 --> 00:41:11.260
-Mark Twain Award?
-No.

00:41:13.554 --> 00:41:16.015
-You should get the Mark Twain--
-I don't want it.

00:41:17.183 --> 00:41:18.601
You're my Mark Twain Award.

00:41:19.102 --> 00:41:20.269
You and this car.

00:41:20.603 --> 00:41:21.729
[revs engine]

00:41:23.272 --> 00:41:25.191
-People like that.
-Easy, Jerry Seinfeld.

00:41:25.274 --> 00:41:26.526
[both laugh]

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:42.250
Like, I'm gonna meet a couple of friends
of mine this afternoon.